#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# max_limit

#% text
# `max_limit(f, x -> a)` gives the limit superior of `f` as `x` tends to `a`: the largest value that `f` keeps coming back to near `a`.

#% text
# `max_limit(f, x -> a, direction: from_above)` and `direction: from_below` take one side.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# When the limit exists, `max_limit` gives it.

#% item
# A sum of a function with a limit and a bounded oscillation `c sin(u)`, `c cos(u)` with `u` tending to an infinity gives the limit plus the amplitude.

#% item
# At a finite point without a direction, the larger of the two one-sided values is given.

#% item
# A list of rules `[y -> b, x -> a]` takes the limits one after the other, the last rule first.

#% item
# When the value cannot be decided, the call stays unevaluated.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# An oscillation that settles between -1 and 1:

#% cell
max_limit(sin(x) - 1/x, x -> inf)
#% output
#= 1

#% text
# A pole of odd order: the values grow without bound on one side:

#% cell
max_limit(1/x, x -> 0)
#% output
#= inf

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# The amplitude of a combination of sine and cosine:

#% cell
max_limit(3*sin(x) + 4*cos(x) + 1, x -> inf)
#% output
#= 6

#% text
# Iterated limits, the last rule first:

#% cell
[max_limit(y/(x + y), [y -> 0, x -> 0]), max_limit(y/(x + y), [x -> 0, y -> 0])]
#% output
#= [1, 0]

#% subsection
# Options

#% text
# One side of the point:

#% cell
max_limit(x/(x^3 + x^2), x -> 0, direction: from_below)
#% output
#= -inf

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# Where the limit exists, `max_limit`, `min_limit` and `limit` agree:

#% cell
[max_limit(sqrt(x^2 + 3*x) - sqrt(x^2 + 2*x), x -> inf), min_limit(sqrt(x^2 + 3*x) - sqrt(x^2 + 2*x), x -> inf)]
#% output
#= [1/2, 1/2]

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# A growing oscillation reaches both infinities:

#% cell
[max_limit(x*sin(x), x -> inf), min_limit(x*sin(x), x -> inf)]
#% output
#= [inf, -inf]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `min_limit`, `limit`, `discrete_limit`
